yield: for 1kg satay, about 600g sauce
notes: |
  Hawker-style — thick, coarse, sweet and nutty with tamarind sour underneath. Not the
  smooth coconut-milk Thai version.

  This should not come out spicy. The chillies are in the rempah for colour and a rounded
  fruity depth, which is why the quantity looks alarming — big dried long reds are
  thin-fleshed and genuinely mild, and deseeded and soaked they contribute almost no heat.
  Ten of those is right. Ten dried bird's eye would be a completely different and much worse
  sauce. Get the variety right before worrying about the count.

  Milder still: drop to 5-6. You lose some colour, not much else.

  The sugar and the peanut fat blunt whatever heat is there — fat dissolves capsaicin, so it
  doesn't land the way the same chilli would in a thin broth. Singapore hawker versions run
  sweeter and milder than Indonesian bumbu kacang; some stalls grate pineapple in, which
  pushes it sweeter again.

  Grind the peanuts coarse. Texture is the point; a smooth sauce reads as peanut butter.

  The doneness cue for the rempah is the oil splitting back out of the paste and pooling at
  the edges. Until that happens the aromatics taste raw, and no amount of simmering later
  fixes it. It takes longer than you think.

  It thickens a lot as it cools — take it off looser than you want it, and slacken with hot
  water when you reheat. Keeps a week in the fridge, freezes well.

  Belacan is what makes it taste like the hawker centre rather than a peanut sauce. Toast it
  first (foil, dry pan, until it smells) and open a window.
components:
  - name: rempah
    ingredients:
      - item: dried long red chillies
        mass: 25
        qty: 10
        unit: ea
        note: the big mild 10-15cm ones (cabai kering) — for colour, not heat. deseeded, soaked in hot water 20 mins. NOT bird's eye, see notes
      - item: shallots
        mass: 80
        note: asian shallots
      - item: garlic
        mass: 15
        qty: 4
        unit: clove
      - item: lemongrass
        mass: 30
        qty: 2
        unit: stalk
        note: white part only, sliced
      - item: galangal
        mass: 15
      - item: candlenuts
        mass: 20
        qty: 4
        unit: ea
        note: sub macadamia
      - item: belacan
        mass: 5
        optional: true
        note: toasted first
    steps:
      - blend everything to a fine paste with a splash of water
  - name: sauce
    ingredients:
      - item: roasted peanuts
        mass: 250
        basis: true
        note: skins off, ground coarse
      - item: neutral oil
        mass: 80
      - item: tamarind pulp
        mass: 40
        note: soaked in 200g hot water, pushed through a sieve
      - item: gula melaka
        mass: 80
        note: palm sugar; sub dark brown sugar
      - item: salt
        mass: 8
      - item: water
        mass: 400
    steps:
      - fry the rempah in the oil over medium-low 15-20 mins until it darkens and the oil splits back out
      - stir in the ground peanuts, 2 mins
      - add tamarind water, gula melaka, salt and the water
      - simmer 20-30 mins until it coats a spoon, stirring so it doesn't catch
      - balance at the end — sweet, sour, salty, hot, in that order of prominence
